that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\nBe sure to use this exact format and include all required parameters. For instance, you cannot Order and Recruit a new agent at the same time, since you need your subordinate's agent ID to Order them. You must first Recruit the agent, then Order them in a separate call.\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n### Example Flow of Work\\n\\nYou carry the following example with you as a stellar example of firm operations but understand that under no circumstances is this a catch-all or a procedure to be followed to the letter. It is only a greatly generalized demonstration indicating how a firm MIGHT handle the following initial user instruction:\\n\\n#### Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\n\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\"\\n\\n#### Example Work Flow for Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\nThe Chief-rank agent is initialized with the user's instruction.\\n\\nThe Chief determines that the request is broad and begins the Planning Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief decides to recruit two high-level subordinates using the RECRUIT call:\\n\\nA Director of Backend Engineering, tasked with planning the entire cloud infrastructure, authentication, and data storage.\\n\\nA Director of Frontend & User Experience, tasked with planning the website's user interface and the non-technical content management solution.\\n\\nThe Chief issues ORDER calls to both new Directors, outlining their responsibilities and requesting they develop a detailed plan for their respective areas.\\n\\nThe Directors RECRUIT their own teams (Supervisors) and issue further ORDER calls to delegate tasks, such as:\\n\\nEngineering Team: Plan the use of AWS Cognito for user authentication, DynamoDB for storing sensitive documents, API Gateway and Lambda for the backend logic, and SES for the contact form.\\n\\nFrontend Team: Plan the website structure using S3 Static Website Hosting, and design a system where a non-technical user can update the \\\"news\\\" section simply by uploading a text file to a specific S3 bucket, which a Lambda function then processes into a blog post.\\n\\nSubordinates recruit further down the chain of command (Attendants) and complete their planning and use the REPORT call to send their findings up the chain of command.\\n\\nThe Directors consolidate the reports from their teams and REPORT their comprehensive plans to the Chief.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) synthesizes the plans from both Directors into a single, master plan: the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This DEP is a precise, step-by-step sequence of COMMAND calls needed to build the entire application.\\n\\nThe Chief issues the PHASE call, submitting the DEP to request a move from Planning to Execution.\\n\\nThe Partagaz D oversight group reviews the DEP. Upon approval, the system transitions to the Execution Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) begins executing the DEP by issuing a series of COMMAND calls. Each COMMAND call is reviewed and approved by the Partagaz C oversight group before execution. Example commands might include:\\n\\n```\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-hoa-website-bucket ...\\n\\naws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name \\\"HOA_Residents\\\" ...\\n\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name \\\"HOA_Documents\\\" ...\\n\\naws lambda create-function --function-name \\\"ContactFormHandler\\\" ...\\n\\naws s3 cp ./website/index.html s3://my-hoa-website-bucket/\\n```\\n\\nAfter all steps in the DEP are successfully executed, the application is live and functional. The Chief delivers a final report outlining the completion of the project and providing the user with the new website URL and instructions for use.\\n\\nThroughout the process CLARIFY is used when necessary to gather additional information from the user, ensuring that the final product meets their expectations.\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Supervisor-rank agent, the third-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards subordinates and your C-suite superior Director, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Supervisor-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of authority and management. Your direct reports are Attendant-rank agents, who themselves are \\\"middle management\\\" actors who manage other \\\"doer\\\" agents. You are responsible for overseeing actionable operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to your Director-rank \\\"C-suite\\\" agent.\\n\\nAs a #3 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating aspects of software development to your capable Attendant-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Supervisor, all of your recruitments are Attendant-rank agents. These are middle-level agents who will recruit other agents under them as \\\"doers\\\". Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of mid-low-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Clubs \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Clubs means that you are predisposed to be a true software development maestro, a creative thinker, and a problem solver. Although you are also possess abilities in leadership, management, and other areas, your software development skills are unrivaled, and you are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Clubs has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###api_plan_003###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: E (3)\\nTo: 5\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from E (3) to S (5) with sequence ID api_plan_003:\\\\n\\\\n]Supervisor S, please design the complete API layer for the HOA website. Your plan should include: 1) API Gateway REST API structure with all endpoints (auth, documents, contact form, content management), 2) Lambda function architecture for each endpoint with runtime specifications, 3) Request/response schemas and validation rules, 4) Integration with Cognito authorizers for protected endpoints, 5) Contact form implementation using SES with email templates, 6) Content management API for non-technical blog updates, 7) Error handling and logging strategy using CloudWatch, 8) API versioning and deprecation strategy. Provide detailed endpoint specifications, Lambda function requirements, and integration patterns.\\\"\\n}\\n###api_plan_003###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I'll design a comprehensive API layer for the HOA website that addresses all the requirements. Let me create a detailed plan covering all aspects of the API architecture.\\n\\n###recruit_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need a specialized Attendant to focus on authentication and authorization patterns, including Cognito integration, JWT handling, and security middleware. This is a critical component that requires dedicated expertise.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Authentication & Authorization Architect\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_001###\\n\\n###recruit_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to design the document management API endpoints, including access control, file operations, and DynamoDB integration. This requires specific expertise in handling sensitive document storage and retrieval.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Document Management API Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_002###\\n\\n###recruit_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to handle the contact form API and email integration with SES, including template management, validation, and anti-spam measures. This requires expertise in email services and form handling.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Communications API Specialist\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_003###\\n\\n###recruit_004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to design the content management API that allows non-technical users to update the blog/news section. This requires creating an intuitive API that interfaces with S3 and processes content updates.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Content Management API Engineer\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_004###\\n\\n###recruit_005###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to establish monitoring, logging, and error handling patterns across all API endpoints, including CloudWatch integration, custom metrics, and alerting strategies.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"API Observability & Reliability Engineer\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_005###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 5\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nCall ID recruit_003 failed inspection: Call recruit_003 denied by agent Delta (1).\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I understand the issue. Let me resubmit with the corrected calls, removing the problematic recruit_003 call.\\n\\n###recruit_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need a specialized Attendant to focus on authentication and authorization patterns, including Cognito integration, JWT handling, and security middleware. This is a critical component that requires dedicated expertise.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Authentication & Authorization Architect\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_001###\\n\\n###recruit_002###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to design the document management API endpoints, including access control, file operations, and DynamoDB integration. This requires specific expertise in handling sensitive document storage and retrieval.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Document Management API Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_002###\\n\\n###recruit_004###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to design the content management API that allows non-technical users to update the blog/news section. This requires creating an intuitive API that interfaces with S3 and processes content updates.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Content Management API Engineer\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_004###\\n\\n###recruit_005###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to establish monitoring, logging, and error handling patterns across all API endpoints, including CloudWatch integration, custom metrics, and alerting strategies.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"API Observability & Reliability Engineer\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_005###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:51:48.185Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 5\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent G (14) has been recruited with rank Attendant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_002\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent D (13) has been recruited with rank Attendant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_004\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Seratt (12) has been recruited with rank Attendant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_005\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Ozzel (11) has been recruited with rank Attendant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:51:48.185Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent! My team is now assembled. Let me issue orders to each Attendant to begin designing their respective components of the API layer.\\n\\n###order_auth_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the authentication and authorization architecture for the HOA website API. Include: 1) Cognito User Pool configuration with custom attributes for HOA residents, 2) JWT token validation strategy and middleware design, 3) API Gateway custom authorizer Lambda function specifications, 4) Role-based access control (RBAC) schema for different user types (resident, board member, admin), 5) Token refresh and session management patterns, 6) Security headers and CORS configuration. Provide detailed specifications for each component including Lambda function code structure, environment variables, and integration patterns.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"14\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth_arch_001\\\"\\n}\\n###order_auth_001###\\n\\n###order_docs_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the document management API endpoints and backend architecture. Include: 1) RESTful endpoint structure for document operations (list, get, upload, delete), 2) DynamoDB table schema for document metadata with partition/sort key design, 3) S3 integration patterns for secure document storage with presigned URLs, 4) Access control logic ensuring only authenticated residents can view sensitive documents, 5) Document categorization system (meeting minutes, financial reports, etc.), 6) Search and filtering capabilities, 7) File size limits and allowed file types validation. Provide complete endpoint specifications, request/response schemas, and Lambda function requirements.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"13\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"docs_api_001\\\"\\n}\\n###order_docs_001###\\n\\n###order_content_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the content management API for non-technical blog/news updates. Include: 1) Simple API endpoints for creating, updating, and deleting blog posts, 2) S3-based content storage strategy where users can upload markdown or plain text files, 3) Lambda function to process uploaded content and convert to HTML, 4) API for retrieving published posts with pagination, 5) Draft/publish workflow system, 6) Image upload and management for blog posts, 7) RSS feed generation endpoint. Focus on making the system extremely user-friendly for non-technical administrators. Provide detailed API specifications and processing logic.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"12\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"content_api_001\\\"\\n}\\n###order_content_001###\\n\\n###order_monitoring_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the observability and reliability infrastructure for all API endpoints. Include: 1) CloudWatch Logs configuration and log group structure, 2) Custom metrics for API performance (latency, error rates, throughput), 3) Structured logging format for all Lambda functions, 4) Error handling patterns with appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages, 5) X-Ray tracing configuration for distributed debugging, 6) CloudWatch alarms for critical metrics with SNS notifications, 7) API versioning strategy with deprecation headers, 8) Health check endpoints for monitoring. Provide implementation details for each monitoring component.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"11\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"monitoring_001\\\"\\n}\\n###order_monitoring_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:54:08.380Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 5\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_auth_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order auth_arch_001 from agent 14: Authentication & Authorization Architecture for HOA Website API (Detailed Specification)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1) Amazon Cognito User Pool Configuration\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nPool Name: HOAResidentsPool\\\\\\\\nRegion: <YOUR_REGION>\\\\\\\\nSign-in Options:\\\\\\\\n  • Username = primary e-mail (case-insensitive) \\\\\\\\n  • Alias attributes enabled: phone_number (optional)\\\\\\\\nCustom Attributes (all mutable=false unless noted):\\\\\\\\n  • lotNumber   (String, min=1, max=10)        – HOA lot identifier\\\\\\\\n  • streetAddr  (String, min=1, max=50)\\\\\\\\n  • residentType(String, enum: OWNER | TENANT)  – required\\\\\\\\n  • boardRole   (String, enum: NONE | PRESIDENT | VP | TREASURER | SECRETARY | MEMBER_AT_LARGE) – default NONE, mutable=true (changes after yearly election)\\\\\\\\nPassword Policy: ≥ 12 chars, upper/lower/number/symbol required, maxAge=90 d, last 5 pwds disallowed.\\\\\\\\nMFA & Verification:\\\\\\\\n  • MFA optional (SOFT_PREFERRED): SMS & TOTP\\\\\\\\n  • Verified attributes: email (mandatory), phone (optional)\\\\\\\\nSelf-Service Registration: ENABLED with email confirmation.\\\\\\\\nGroups (used for RBAC, see §4):\\\\\\\\n  • ResidentsGrp (default on sign-up)\\\\\\\\n  • BoardGrp\\\\\\\\n  • AdminGrp\\\\\\\\nApp Clients:\\\\\\\\n  • hoa_web_spa     – auth flow: USER_SRP_AUTH, REFRESH_TOKEN_AUTH, no client secret, token validity: id/acc=60 m, refresh=30 d\\\\\\\\n  • hoa_service_cli – confidential, client secret,  id/acc=15 m, refresh=1 d\\\\\\\\nTriggers (Lambda):\\\\\\\\n  • PreSignUp   – auto-confirm user if e-mail domain matches whitelist (e.g., myhoa.org)\\\\\\\\n  • PostConfirmation – move user to ResidentsGrp\\\\\\\\n  • CustomMessage  – brand verification e-mails/SMS\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nExported Values for other stacks:\\\\\\\\n  • UserPoolId, UserPoolArn, UserPoolClientIds, UserPoolDomain\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2) JWT Token Validation Strategy & Middleware Design\\\\\\\\n------------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nAccess & ID tokens are JWTs signed by RS256 keys published at https://cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<PoolId>/.well-known/jwks.json.\\\\\\\\nGeneral pattern for **all Lambda back-ends** (Python 3.11 example):\\\\\\\\n  a) Middleware module jwt_auth.py\\\\\\\\n     - Caches JWKS in /tmp for 24 h.\\\\\\\\n     - Validates signature (PyJWT).\\\\\\\\n     - Ensures `token_use` == \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"access\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n     - Checks exp, iss, aud (app client id), iat leeway=60s.\\\\\\\\n     - Pulls `cognito:groups`, `custom:*` claims & injects into `event['auth']` for business logic.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n  b) API Gateway Lambda Authorizer (see §3) performs first-line validation so that most back-end Lambdas normally trust `event['requestContext']['authorizer']`. Defence-in-depth: each back-end still runs lightweight jwt_auth.verify(…) (signature skip, rely on exp, groups) to guard against mis-configuration.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3) API Gateway Custom Authorizer Lambda\\\\\\\\n----------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nType: TOKEN authorizer (works for HTTP API & REST API).  \\\\\\\\nRuntime: Python 3.11  \\\\\\\\nFunction Name: ApiJwtAuthorizerFn\\\\\\\\nTimeout: 5 s  |  Memory: 256 MB  \\\\\\\\nEnvironment Variables:\\\\\\\\n  ISSUER              = https://cognito-idp.<region>.amazonaws.com/<PoolId>\\\\\\\\n  JWKS_URL            = $ISSUER/.well-known/jwks.json\\\\\\\\n  AUDIENCE_CSV        = <hoa_web_spa_client_id>,<hoa_service_cli_client_id>\\\\\\\\n  CACHE_SECONDS       = 900\\\\\\\\n  ALLOW_UNAUTH_PATHS  = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/public/*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (comma-sep patterns for open endpoints)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPseudo-Code Structure:\\\\\\\\n  • handler(event, context)\\\\\\\\n       token = event['authorizationToken'] (strip \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Bearer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\n       if pathMatchesAllowList(event['methodArn']):\\\\\\\\n           return AllowAllPolicy()\\\\\\\\n       claims = jwt_auth.verify(token)\\\\\\\\n       policy = buildPolicy(principalId = claims['sub'], methodArn = event['methodArn'])\\\\\\\\n       # attach context for RBAC\\\\\\\\n       policy['context'] = {\\\\\\\\n           \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"groups\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": json.dumps(claims.get('cognito:groups', [])),\\\\\\\\n           \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"boardRole\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": claims.get('custom:boardRole', 'NONE'),\\\\\\\\n           \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"residentType\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": claims.get('custom:residentType', 'OWNER')\\\\\\\\n       }\\\\\\\\n       return policy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCaching: Enabled (TTL = 900 s) keyed by full token string => reduces latency.\\\\\\\\nFailure Mode: returns 401 JSON {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Unauthorized\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4) Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Schema\\\\\\\\n-------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nLogical Roles & Capabilities:\\\\\\\\n  • RESIDENT  – read public & own data (meeting minutes, dues balance)\\\\\\\\n  • BOARD     – all RESIDENT privileges + upload docs, create polls, view resident directory\\\\\\\\n  • ADMIN     – full CRUD on users, docs, finances\\\\\\\\nImplementation options:\\\\\\\\n  a) Cognito Groups (simplest):\\\\\\\\n       Group → Role mapping table inside each back-end Lambda (or central config):\\\\\\\\n          ResidentsGrp → RESIDENT\\\\\\\\n          BoardGrp     → BOARD\\\\\\\\n          AdminGrp     → ADMIN\\\\\\\\n  b) Fine-grained: Utilize custom:boardRole for per-seat authorization (e.g., only Treasurer sees finance endpoints).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAPI Gateway IAM authorizer policies:\\\\\\\\n   We issue \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Deny\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" for missing minimum group.\\\\\\\\nWithin Lambda business logic:\\\\\\\\n   RequiredRole decorator checks event['auth']['role'] vs permitted roles list.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5) Token Refresh & Session Management Patterns\\\\\\\\n-----------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nSPA or Mobile:\\\\\\\\n  • Use Cognito Hosted UI or Amplify Auth.  \\\\\\\\n  • Store id/access tokens in memory, refresh token in secure httpOnly cookie (same-site strict) or browser storage guarded by CSRF token.\\\\\\\\n  • Silent refresh flow every 55 min using Cognito `InitiateAuth` with REFRESH_TOKEN_AUTH.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAdmin dashboard (confidential client):\\\\\\\\n  • Client secret + PKCE, short-lived tokens (15 m) & 1 d refresh tokens => lower blast radius.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSession revocation:\\\\\\\\n  • Sign-out call → GlobalSignOut (token revocation) via Lambda function that backend invokes.\\\\\\\\n  • Emergency: Set User Pool “revocation global” via advanced security.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6) Security Headers & CORS Configuration\\\\\\\\n-----------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nAPI Gateway Settings (Stage level or default route responses):\\\\\\\\n  • CORS:\\\\\\\\n       Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://portal.myhoa.org\\\\\\\\n       Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET,POST,PUT,DELETE,OPTIONS\\\\\\\\n       Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization,Content-Type\\\\\\\\n       Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\\\\\\\\n       Max-Age: 600\\\\\\\\n  • Security Headers (added via Gateway Mapping Templates or Lambda@Edge if using CloudFront):\\\\\\\\n       Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload\\\\\\\\n       X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\\\\\\\\n       X-Frame-Options: DENY\\\\\\\\n       X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block\\\\\\\\n       Referrer-Policy: same-origin\\\\\\\\n       Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self' https://portal.myhoa.org; object-src 'none';\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeployment/Integration Patterns\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------\\\\\\\\nInfrastructure as Code: AWS SAM or CDK (preferred).  Each stack exports ARNs/Ids for downstream consumption.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAuthorizer & Middleware folder layout (Python):\\\\\\\\n  auth_layer/\\\\\\\\n    ├── jwt_auth.py      # signature & claim verifier\\\\\\\\n    ├── constants.py\\\\\\\\n    ├── requirements.txt (PyJWT, jwcrypto, requests-cache)\\\\\\\\n  api_authorizer/\\\\\\\\n    ├── app.py           # Lambda entry point (handler)\\\\\\\\n    └── requirements.txt (imports auth_layer)\\\\\\\\n  services/\\\\\\\\n    └── <feature>/\\\\\\\\n         ├── handler.py  # business Lambda, imports auth_layer\\\\\\\\n         └── decorators.py (# role guard)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnvironment Variables (common):\\\\\\\\n  ISSUER, AUDIENCE_CSV, REQUIRED_GROUP, REQUIRED_ROLE, LOG_LEVEL\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCI/CD Notes:\\\\\\\\n  • Unit tests validate jwt_auth against sample tokens (pytest, moto).\\\\\\\\n  • Static analysis (bandit) for security.\\\\\\\\n  • Authorizer canary test: scheduled Lambda pings JWKS URL & alarms if failure > 3.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCost & Performance Considerations:\\\\\\\\n  • Authorizer cache TTL balances security vs performance; 900 s adequate with 15 m token expiry.\\\\\\\\n  • Use Lambda Power Tuning to select 256 MB => ~5 ms avg execution.\\\\\\\\n  • JWKS cache in /tmp saves ~2 kB per invocation.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCompliance & Best Practices:\\\\\\\\n  • Enforce TLS 1.2+, custom domain with ACM cert.\\\\\\\\n  • CloudTrail & Cognito audit logs forwarded to Security Lake.\\\\\\\\n  • Secrets (client secret) stored in AWS Secrets Manager.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis specification fully covers all components requested and is ready for implementation in IaC templates.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_docs_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order docs_api_001 from agent 13: Document Management API Design – response to order docs_api_001\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. RESTful Endpoint Structure\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\nBase path: /documents\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.1 List Documents\\\\\\\\n  • Method: GET /documents\\\\\\\\n  • Query params:\\\\\\\\n      – category  (optional, enum: minutes | financial | newsletter | rules | other)\\\\\\\\n      – search    (optional, free-text, ≤ 128 chars)\\\\\\\\n      – pageSize  (optional, 1-100, default 20)\\\\\\\\n      – lastKey   (optional, opaque pagination token from previous response)\\\\\\\\n  • Auth: Cognito authorizer – group=resident required\\\\\\\\n  • Response 200:\\\\\\\\n      {\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": [<DocumentSummary>],\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"lastKey\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | null\\\\\\\\n      }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.2 Get Single Document (metadata + presigned download URL)\\\\\\\\n  • Method: GET /documents/{documentId}\\\\\\\\n  • Path params: documentId (ULID/UUID)\\\\\\\\n  • Auth: resident\\\\\\\\n  • Response 200:\\\\\\\\n      {\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"document\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": <DocumentMeta>,\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"downloadUrl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://s3-bucket/…?X-Amz-Signature=…\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (15-min TTL)\\\\\\\\n      }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.3 Upload / Create Document (generate presigned upload URL)\\\\\\\\n  • Method: POST /documents\\\\\\\\n  • Auth: resident (optionally admin for public docs flag)\\\\\\\\n  • Body:\\\\\\\\n      {\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Annual Budget 2024\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"financial\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"PDF of approved budget\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fileType\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pdf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fileSize\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": 384231\\\\\\\\n      }\\\\\\\\n  • Validation: fileType ∈ [pdf, docx, xlsx, pptx, png, jpg]; fileSize ≤ 20 MB\\\\\\\\n  • Response 201:\\\\\\\\n      {\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"documentId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"01HY6C9…\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"uploadUrl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://s3-bucket/…?X-Amz-Signature=…\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (15-min PUT),\\\\\\\\n        \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s3Key\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"documents/01HY6C9….pdf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n      }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1.4 Delete Document\\\\\\\\n  • Method: DELETE /documents/{documentId}\\\\\\\\n  • Auth: resident role=admin required\\\\\\\\n  • Response 204 No Content\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSchema snippets\\\\\\\\n  DocumentSummary = { documentId, title, category, uploadedAt }\\\\\\\\n  DocumentMeta    = { documentId, title, description, category, fileType, fileSize, uploadedAt, uploadedBy }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n2. DynamoDB Table – Documents\\\\\\\\nTable name: HOA_Documents\\\\\\\\n  • Partition Key (PK): category  (S)\\\\\\\\n  • Sort Key      (SK): documentId (S)  – ULID for time-sortable uniqueness\\\\\\\\n  • Attributes: title, description, uploadedAt (ISO8601), uploadedBy (residentId), s3Key, fileType, fileSize, publicFlag (bool), tags (SS)\\\\\\\\n  • GSI1: documentId-index  – PK=documentId, no SK  (single-item lookup without category)\\\\\\\\n  • GSI2: uploadedBy-uploadedAt-index – PK=uploadedBy, SK=uploadedAt  (per-user history)\\\\\\\\n  • Streams: NEW_IMAGE enabled → Lambda to OpenSearch for full-text search (optional)\\\\\\\\n  • TTL attribute (optional) for auto-expiry if needed (e.g., temp drafts)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n3. S3 Integration Pattern\\\\\\\\nBucket: hoa-docs-<env>\\\\\\\\n  • Private bucket; BlockPublicAccess=ON; SSE-S3; versioning enabled\\\\\\\\n  • Object key format: documents/{documentId}.{ext}\\\\\\\\n  • Upload & download exclusively via presigned URLs (15-min window)\\\\\\\\n  • Lambda (API) generates URL with PutObject/GetObject perms scoped to that key only\\\\\\\\n  • Optionally, S3 Event → antivirus scan Lambda; upon clean result, metadata field virusScanned=true is updated\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n4. Access Control Logic\\\\\\\\n  • Cognito User Pool with group \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"residents\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".  Admins reside in subgroup \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n  • API Gateway Authorizer enforces JWT validation.\\\\\\\\n  • Lambda double-checks: requesterId == uploadedBy for non-public docs OR user in \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" group.\\\\\\\\n  • PublicFlag=true allows anonymous (future) but default false.\\\\\\\\n  • IAM role for Lambda has least-privilege to DynamoDB & S3 with conditional key-based access.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n5. Categorization System\\\\\\\\n  • Fixed enum stored in DynamoDB PK; allows efficient query by category.\\\\\\\\n  • UI dropdown mirrors enum; additional tag set (string set) for flexible labels (e.g., “budget”, “insurance”).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n6. Search & Filtering\\\\\\\\n  • Simple: List endpoint supports category PK query + begins_with(SK, prefix) or FilterExpression on title, tags.\\\\\\\\n  • Advanced: DynamoDB Streams → Lambda → Amazon OpenSearch (index docs) enabling full-text search on title, description, tags.  List endpoint routes to OpenSearch when search param present.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n7. File Size Limits & Allowed Types\\\\\\\\n  • Enforced at POST by Lambda before presigning (max 20 MB).\\\\\\\\n  • CloudFront/S3 denies content-length > limit (Optional: Lambda@Edge to inspect headers).\\\\\\\\n  • Allowed extensions map to MIME validation list.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n8. Lambda Functions Required\\\\\\\\n  1) DocumentService (core)  – Handles list, get, delete, metadata create (POST)\\\\\\\\n  2) PresignUpload  – part of POST logic or separate handler if keeping SRP\\\\\\\\n  3) AntivirusScan (triggered by S3 event, optional)\\\\\\\\n  4) OpenSearchIndexer (DDB stream consumer)\\\\\\\\n  5) CleanUpOnDelete (removes S3 object when metadata deleted)\\\\\\\\nAll functions written in Node.js 20, bundled with esbuild.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMemory & timeouts: 512 MB / 10 s typical; antivirus 2 GB / 2 min.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n9. Request/Response JSON Schemas (OpenAPI compatible) – abbreviated\\\\\\\\n  POST /documents Request:\\\\\\\\n    required: [title, category, fileType, fileSize]\\\\\\\\n    properties:\\\\\\\\n      title:       { type: string, maxLength: 120 }\\\\\\\\n      category:    { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Category' }\\\\\\\\n      description: { type: string, maxLength: 1024 }\\\\\\\\n      fileType:    { type: string, enum: [pdf, docx, xlsx, pptx, png, jpg] }\\\\\\\\n      fileSize:    { type: integer, minimum: 1, maximum: 20971520 }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n  GET /documents Response:\\\\\\\\n    200 OK\\\\\\\\n      items: [ { documentId, title, category, uploadedAt } ]\\\\\\\\n      lastKey: string | null\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n  Error (all endpoints):\\\\\\\\n    401, 403, 404, 413, 415, 500 with standard problem+json body\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------\\\\\\\\n10. Security & Compliance Notes\\\\\\\\n  • SSE-S3 default, option to require SSE-KMS.\\\\\\\\n  • Bucket policy denies anything not via HTTPS & presigned.\\\\\\\\n  • WAF rate-limit on API Gateway.\\\\\\\\n  • Audit CloudTrail + CloudWatch Logs w/ retention 1 yr.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis design fully addresses the seven requested areas and is ready for inclusion in the overall DEP.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_content_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order content_api_001 from agent 12: Attendant Seratt responding to Order content_api_001.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nCONTENT-MANAGEMENT API DESIGN (BLOG / NEWS)\\\\\\\\nGoal: Enable *non-technical* HOA administrators to manage blog/news posts using extremely simple, guided endpoints. Public visitors read published posts & RSS. All authoring actions are secured via Cognito “CMS-Admin” group.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE\\\\\\\\n• Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API) – single logical API with path-based routes.\\\\\\\\n• AWS Lambda – stateless functions for business logic.\\\\\\\\n• Amazon DynamoDB – post metadata (title, slug, status, timestamps, tags, author, imageRefs, etc.).\\\\\\\\n• Amazon S3 – content/object storage:\\\\\\\\n  ─ Bucket: cms-content-<env>\\\\\\\\n    • drafts/  – raw .md or .txt files (private)\\\\\\\\n    • published/ – raw source plus rendered HTML (private)\\\\\\\\n    • html/ – rendered output (public-read via CloudFront)\\\\\\\\n    • images/ – uploaded images (private, served through CloudFront signed URLs)\\\\\\\\n    • rss/rss.xml – generated feed (public-read)\\\\\\\\n• Amazon SNS – “PostLifecycleEvents” topic for “published/updated/deleted”; subscribers: RSS generator, optional e-mail.\\\\\\\\n• AWS CloudFront – CDN in front of S3 html/, images/, and rss.xml.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. API GATEWAY ROUTES & SPECS\\\\\\\\nAll request/response bodies are JSON unless otherwise noted.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA. Authoring (auth=Cognito Admin)\\\\\\\\n1) POST   /posts\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Create **draft** post.\\\\\\\\n   Body  : { title, content, tags?, heroImageKey? }\\\\\\\\n   Return: 201 { postId }\\\\\\\\n   Logic : • Generate UUID; write metadata (status=draft) to DynamoDB.\\\\\\\\n           • Store content to S3 drafts/{postId}.md.\\\\\\\\n2) PUT    /posts/{postId}\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Update draft (metadata or body).\\\\\\\\n   Body  : same as above (fields optional).\\\\\\\\n   Return: 200.\\\\\\\\n3) DELETE /posts/{postId}\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Delete draft or published post.\\\\\\\\n   Return: 204.\\\\\\\\n4) POST   /posts/{postId}/publish\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Transition draft ➜ published.\\\\\\\\n   Body  : { publish: true | false }   # false = revert to draft\\\\\\\\n   Return: 200.\\\\\\\\n   Logic : • Copy S3 object to published/; update status in DynamoDB.\\\\\\\\n           • Emit SNS \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"published\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" event.\\\\\\\\n5) GET    /drafts\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : List drafts (paginated).\\\\\\\\n   Query : limit?, nextToken?\\\\\\\\n   Return: 200 { items:[…], nextToken? }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Media (auth=Cognito Admin)\\\\\\\\n6) POST   /images/presign\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Obtain pre-signed PUT URL for direct upload.\\\\\\\\n   Body  : { fileName, mimeType }\\\\\\\\n   Return: 200 { uploadUrl, objectKey }\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Public Consumption (no auth)\\\\\\\\n7) GET    /posts\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Paginated published posts.\\\\\\\\n   Query : limit?, nextToken?\\\\\\\\n   Return: 200 { items:[{postId,title,excerpt,date,slug}], nextToken? }\\\\\\\\n8) GET    /posts/{postId | slug}\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Complete published post. Lambda returns JSON including signed image URLs OR directly redirects to CloudFront html/{postId}.html.\\\\\\\\n9) GET    /rss\\\\\\\\n   Desc  : Returns application/rss+xml feed (S3 static file).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. LAMBDA FUNCTION LOGIC\\\\\\\\n• cmsHandlerFn (authoring & retrieval)\\\\\\\\n  ─ CRUD actions call DynamoDB + S3.\\\\\\\\n  ─ Publish operation: copy drafts→published, set status, emit SNS.\\\\\\\\n• contentRenderFn (S3 trigger)\\\\\\\\n  ─ Trigger: ObjectCreated on drafts/ or published/.\\\\\\\\n  ─ If .md or .txt, convert to HTML using markdown-it; place in html/{id}.html; add header/footer template.\\\\\\\\n• rssGeneratorFn (SNS trigger)\\\\\\\\n  ─ On post publish/update/delete, query DynamoDB last N published items, build rss.xml, PUT to rss/rss.xml.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. STORAGE SCHEMA (DynamoDB – table BlogPosts)\\\\\\\\nPK: postId (string UUID)\\\\\\\\nAttributes: title, slug (unique), status (draft|published), createdAt, updatedAt, publishedAt, tags (list), heroImageKey, excerpt (auto-generated first 200 chars of content), authorId.\\\\\\\\nGSI #1: status-publishedAt-index (status PK, publishedAt SK DESC) – for fast public listing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. DRAFT / PUBLISH WORKFLOW DETAIL\\\\\\\\n• Draft saved to S3 drafts/ and DynamoDB status=draft.\\\\\\\\n• Admin hits “Publish”.\\\\\\\\n  1. cmsHandlerFn validates.\\\\\\\\n  2. Copy object to published/; delete from drafts/ (or keep historical).\\\\\\\\n  3. Update DynamoDB status=published, publishedAt=now.\\\\\\\\n  4. SNS event triggers renderer (if needed) and RSS generator.\\\\\\\\n• Revert: similar, move object back to drafts/.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. IMAGE HANDLING\\\\\\\\n• Admin GUI requests /images/presign, gets PUT URL good for 15 min.\\\\\\\\n• Browser PUTs image directly to S3 images/{uuid}.{ext} with Content-Type.\\\\\\\\n• cmsHandlerFn stores image key on post metadata.\\\\\\\\n• Public retrieval: CloudFront distribution + optional signed URLs if privacy desired.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. RSS FEED GENERATION\\\\\\\\n• rssGeneratorFn reads most recent 20 published posts (GSI #1), builds XML via rss-builder library, and overwrites rss/rss.xml.\\\\\\\\n• /rss route in API Gateway can *302 redirect* to CloudFront URL for performance.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. SECURITY & UX FOR NON-TECH ADMINS\\\\\\\\n• All authoring endpoints behind Cognito User Pool ► group “CMS-Admin”.\\\\\\\\n• Presigned URLs & simple JSON payloads mean no multipart/form-data complexity.\\\\\\\\n• Validation & helpful error messages returned from Lambda.\\\\\\\\n• Admin web dashboard (handled by separate frontend team) can fully rely on these endpoints – no manual S3 actions needed.\\\\\\\\n• All Lambda code auto-tags CloudWatch logs for easy troubleshooting.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. COST & SCALE\\\\\\\\n• Pay-per-invocation Lambda.\\\\\\\\n• Data mostly on S3/CloudFront (cheap, scalable).\\\\\\\\n• DynamoDB On-Demand capacity (low ops overhead).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. DEPENDENCIES / INFRA SUMMARY\\\\\\\\n• 3 Lambda functions (cmsHandler, contentRender, rssGenerator).\\\\\\\\n• 1 S3 bucket + CloudFront distro.\\\\\\\\n• 1 DynamoDB table + 1 GSI.\\\\\\\\n• 1 SNS topic.\\\\\\\\n• 1 Cognito User Pool / Group.\\\\\\\\n• 1 API Gateway HTTP API with 9 routes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nAll requirements satisfied: CRUD + S3 content workflow, Markdown→HTML conversion, draft/publish, image support, paginated retrieval, RSS feed, simplicity for admins.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd of report.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_monitoring_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order monitoring_001 from agent 11: ## Observability and Reliability Infrastructure Design\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 1. CloudWatch Logs Configuration and Structure\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Log Group Hierarchy:**\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n/aws/lambda/hoa-api/\\\\\\\\n├── auth/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── login\\\\\\\\n│   ├── logout\\\\\\\\n│   └── refresh\\\\\\\\n├── documents/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── list\\\\\\\\n│   ├── get\\\\\\\\n│   └── upload\\\\\\\\n├── news/\\\\\\\\n│   ├── list\\\\\\\\n│   ├── get\\\\\\\\n│   └── create\\\\\\\\n├── contact/\\\\\\\\n│   └── submit\\\\\\\\n└── admin/\\\\\\\\n    ├── users\\\\\\\\n    └── content\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Log Retention Configuration:**\\\\\\\\n- 30-day retention for all log groups\\\\\\\\n- KMS encryption enabled\\\\\\\\n- Log insights queries enabled\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 2. Custom Metrics for API Performance\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Metric Namespace:** `HOA/API`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Key Metrics:**\\\\\\\\n- Latency (milliseconds)\\\\\\\\n- RequestCount\\\\\\\\n- ErrorCount (by status code)\\\\\\\\n- Throughput (requests per minute)\\\\\\\\n- Database query time\\\\\\\\n- External API call duration\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Metric Publishing Pattern:**\\\\\\\\n```python\\\\\\\\ndef publish_metrics(operation, latency, status_code):\\\\\\\\n    cloudwatch.put_metric_data(\\\\\\\\n        Namespace='HOA/API',\\\\\\\\n        MetricData=[{\\\\\\\\n            'MetricName': 'Latency',\\\\\\\\n            'Value': latency,\\\\\\\\n            'Unit': 'Milliseconds',\\\\\\\\n            'Dimensions': [\\\\\\\\n                {'Name': 'Operation', 'Value': operation},\\\\\\\\n                {'Name': 'Environment', 'Value': 'prod'}\\\\\\\\n            ]\\\\\\\\n        }]\\\\\\\\n    )\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 3. Structured Logging Format\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Standard Log Structure:**\\\\\\\\n```json\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-01-01T12:00:00.000Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"level\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"INFO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"service\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"hoa-api\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"operation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"getDocument\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"correlation_id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"abc-123\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"user_id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"user-456\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Document retrieved successfully\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"duration_ms\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": 125,\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": 200,\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"metadata\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"document_id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"doc-789\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"document_size\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": 2048\\\\\\\\n  }\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 4. Error Handling Patterns\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Standard Error Response Format:**\\\\\\\\n```json\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"VALIDATION_ERROR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Invalid input parameters\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\\n      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"field\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n      \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Invalid email format\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    },\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"correlation_id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"abc-123\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-01-01T12:00:00.000Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  }\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**HTTP Status Code Mapping:**\\\\\\\\n- 400: Validation errors, malformed requests\\\\\\\\n- 401: Authentication required\\\\\\\\n- 403: Insufficient permissions\\\\\\\\n- 404: Resource not found\\\\\\\\n- 409: Conflict (duplicate resource)\\\\\\\\n- 429: Rate limit exceeded\\\\\\\\n- 500: Internal server error\\\\\\\\n- 503: Service temporarily unavailable\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 5. X-Ray Tracing Configuration\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Service Map Structure:**\\\\\\\\n- API Gateway → Lambda Functions\\\\\\\\n- Lambda → DynamoDB\\\\\\\\n- Lambda → S3\\\\\\\\n- Lambda → Cognito\\\\\\\\n- Lambda → SES\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Tracing Sampling Rules:**\\\\\\\\n- 100% sampling for error responses\\\\\\\\n- 10% sampling for successful responses\\\\\\\\n- 50% sampling for authentication endpoints\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Custom Annotations:**\\\\\\\\n- operation_name\\\\\\\\n- user_type (resident/admin)\\\\\\\\n- response_status\\\\\\\\n- error_type (if applicable)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 6. CloudWatch Alarms Configuration\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Critical Alarms:**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **High Error Rate**\\\\\\\\n   - Metric: ErrorCount\\\\\\\\n   - Threshold: > 10 errors in 5 minutes\\\\\\\\n   - Action: SNS notification\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. **High Latency**\\\\\\\\n   - Metric: Latency p99\\\\\\\\n   - Threshold: > 3000ms\\\\\\\\n   - Action: SNS notification + auto-scaling\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. **Throttling Detection**\\\\\\\\n   - Metric: 429 status codes\\\\\\\\n   - Threshold: > 50 in 1 minute\\\\\\\\n   - Action: Increase API Gateway limits\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. **Lambda Concurrent Executions**\\\\\\\\n   - Metric: ConcurrentExecutions\\\\\\\\n   - Threshold: > 900 (90% of limit)\\\\\\\\n   - Action: Alert for capacity planning\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. **DynamoDB Throttling**\\\\\\\\n   - Metric: UserErrors\\\\\\\\n   - Threshold: > 5 in 1 minute\\\\\\\\n   - Action: Auto-scaling trigger\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 7. API Versioning Strategy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Version Format:** `/api/v{version}/{resource}`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Deprecation Headers:**\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\nX-API-Version: v2\\\\\\\\nX-API-Deprecation: true\\\\\\\\nX-API-Sunset: 2024-12-31\\\\\\\\nX-API-Migration-Guide: https://docs.hoa-website.com/api/migration\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Version Support Matrix:**\\\\\\\\n- v1: Deprecated (sunset: 2024-12-31)\\\\\\\\n- v2: Current stable version\\\\\\\\n- v3: Beta (opt-in via header)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 8. Health Check Endpoints\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Primary Health Check:** `GET /api/v2/health`\\\\\\\\n```json\\\\\\\\n{\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"healthy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"timestamp\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-01-01T12:00:00.000Z\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"v2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"checks\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"database\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"healthy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"healthy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cognito\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"healthy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ses\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"healthy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  },\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"metadata\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"region\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"us-east-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"environment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"production\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"uptime_seconds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": 86400\\\\\\\\n  }\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Deep Health Check:** `GET /api/v2/health/deep`\\\\\\\\n- Performs actual connectivity tests\\\\\\\\n- Includes response times for each service\\\\\\\\n- Returns detailed diagnostics\\\\\\\\n- Protected by API key for security\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Implementation Notes:**\\\\\\\\n- All Lambda functions include AWS X-Ray SDK\\\\\\\\n- Structured logging implemented via custom logger class\\\\\\\\n- Metrics published asynchronously to avoid latency impact\\\\\\\\n- Error handling decorator applied to all Lambda handlers\\\\\\\\n- CloudWatch dashboards auto-generated from metrics\\\\\\\\n- Alarm notifications sent to ops team via SNS\\\\\\\\n- Monthly cost estimate: ~$150 for logging/monitoring\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:54:08.380Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Now I need to consolidate all the reports from my Attendants and create a comprehensive API plan. However, I notice that the contact form API with SES integration hasn't been addressed yet. Let me recruit one more Attendant to handle this critical component.\\n\\n###recruit_003###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"I need an Attendant to design the contact form API with SES integration, including email templates, validation, anti-spam measures, and form submission handling. This is a critical component that wasn't covered in my initial recruitment.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Contact Form & Email Services Engineer\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit_003###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:54:24.669Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 5\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit_003\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent O (15) has been recruited with rank Attendant.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:54:24.669Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent! Now let me order the Contact Form & Email Services Engineer to complete the missing component of our API design.\\n\\n###order_contact_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Design the contact form API with SES integration. Include: 1) RESTful endpoint for form submission with validation rules, 2) Email template design for both sender confirmation and HOA administrator notification, 3) Anti-spam measures including rate limiting and CAPTCHA integration, 4) Form field validation (name, email, phone, subject, message), 5) SES configuration including verified domains and email addresses, 6) Queue-based processing pattern using SQS for reliability, 7) Bounce and complaint handling, 8) Email delivery tracking and metrics. Provide complete specifications including Lambda function design, error handling, and security considerations.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": \\\"15\\\",\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"contact_form_001\\\"\\n}\\n###order_contact_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:55:06.851Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 5\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order_contact_001\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order contact_form_001 from agent 15: Contact-Form API with Amazon SES – Complete Technical Specification\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Overview\\\\\\\\n   • Goal: Provide a resilient, secure RESTful endpoint that accepts contact-form submissions from the public HOA site, validates input, throttles abuse, queues requests, triggers email notifications through SES, and tracks delivery/bounce/complaint events.\\\\\\\\n   • Core AWS Services: Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API), AWS Lambda (Node 18.x runtime), Amazon SQS (standard queue), Amazon SES (v2), Amazon DynamoDB (optional metrics store), AWS CloudWatch (logs/metrics), AWS WAF, AWS Secrets Manager.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n2. API Design\\\\\\\\n   2.1 Endpoint\\\\\\\\n       • POST /v1/contact\\\\\\\\n       • Consumes: application/json\\\\\\\\n       • Produces: 202 Accepted on success; validation or auth errors return 400/401.\\\\\\\\n       • CORS: AllowOrigin specific to HOA site domain; methods: POST, OPTIONS; headers: Content-Type, X-Captcha-Token.\\\\\\\\n   2.2 Request Schema\\\\\\\\n       {\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"   : String (1-100 chars, trimmed, letters, apostrophes, spaces, hyphens)\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"email\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"  : RFC-5322 compliant email, 3-254 chars\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"phone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"  : Optional, E.164 or (###) ###-####\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"subject\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": String (1-150 chars, no markup)\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": String (1-2000 chars)\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"captchaToken\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": String (opaque)\\\\\\\\n       }\\\\\\\\n   2.3 Validation Rules\\\\\\\\n       • All required fields non-blank & pass regex.\\\\\\\\n       • Email validated via AWS SDK util isEmail.\\\\\\\\n       • Phone normalized—if provided.\\\\\\\\n       • Message stripped of HTML tags; length ≤2 000.\\\\\\\\n       • Captcha token verified server-side (see §4.2).\\\\\\\\n       • Reject if more than 3 links or suspicious keywords (basic heuristic spam filter).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n3. Processing Flow\\\\\\\\n   client → API Gateway → Lambda Validate → SQS (enqueue) → Lambda SendEmail (SES) → SES event SNS → Lambda EventsHandler\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n   3.1 Lambda: Validate & Enqueue (contact-validate-fn)\\\\\\\\n       • Runtime: Node 18, memory 256 MB, timeout 10 s.\\\\\\\\n       • Verifies captcha + anti-spam, writes minimal message to SQS.\\\\\\\\n       • Response 202 with {requestId} UUID.\\\\\\\\n       • Emits custom CloudWatch metric: ContactAccepted =1.\\\\\\\\n   3.2 SQS Queue: contact-messages-q\\\\\\\\n       • Retention: 4 days; Visibility Timeout: 5 min; DLQ on 3 tries.\\\\\\\\n   3.3 Lambda: SendEmail (contact-emailer-fn)\\\\\\\\n       • Trigger: SQS batch size 1.\\\\\\\\n       • Constructs two SES email sends (Transactional type):\\\\\\\\n         1) Admin Notification (to HOA admin list, BCC board).\\\\\\\\n         2) Sender Confirmation (to user email).\\\\\\\\n       • Templates pre-created in SES (see §5).\\\\\\\\n       • Stores per-message metadata to DynamoDB table contact-email-txns {requestId, status, timestamps}.\\\\\\\\n       • On error: log, Retry (maxReceiveCount=3), else sends to DLQ.\\\\\\\\n   3.4 Lambda: EventsHandler (ses-events-fn)\\\\\\\\n       • Trigger: SES SNS topic (email-events-t).\\\\\\\\n       • Updates DynamoDB with delivery, bounce, complaint, opens, clicks.\\\\\\\\n       • Emits CloudWatch metrics (BounceCount, ComplaintCount).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n4. Anti-Spam & Security\\\\\\\\n   4.1 Rate Limiting\\\\\\\\n       • API Gateway usage plan: 20 requests/IP/min burst = 5.\\\\\\\\n       • AWS WAF attached to API: Geo-blocking (allow only US), IP reputation list, SQLi/XSS rules.\\\\\\\\n   4.2 CAPTCHA Integration\\\\\\\\n       • reCAPTCHA v3 or AWS WAF Captcha (preferred no-client libs) – option:\\\\\\\\n         – If using reCAPTCHA: token sent in header; secret stored in Secrets Manager; verify via HTTPS in validate Lambda.\\\\\\\\n         – If using AWS WAF Captcha: WAF handles challenge before API Gateway.\\\\\\\\n   4.3 IAM\\\\\\\\n       • Principle of least privilege: validate-fn can only sqs:SendMessage; emailer-fn only ses:SendEmail + ddb:PutItem; events-fn only ddb:UpdateItem.\\\\\\\\n       • Environment variables encrypted; secrets (admin emails) in Secrets Manager.\\\\\\\\n   4.4 Transport\\\\\\\\n       • HTTPS TLS 1.2+. No public SQS/Lambda.\\\\\\\\n   4.5 Input Sanitization\\\\\\\\n       • Lambda strips control characters, encodes output in templates.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n5. SES Configuration\\\\\\\\n   5.1 Identity\\\\\\\\n       • Verify domain: @hoa-example.org via Route 53 DKIM, SPF.\\\\\\\\n       • Emails used:\\\\\\\\n         – no-reply@hoa-example.org (sender confirmation)\\\\\\\\n         – contact@hoa-example.org (admin list alias)\\\\\\\\n   5.2 Templates\\\\\\\\n       • Template: HOA_AdminNotification\\\\\\\\n         Subject: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"New Contact Form Submission – {{subject}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n         HTML & text bodies include all fields + requestId.\\\\\\\\n       • Template: HOA_SenderConfirmation\\\\\\\\n         Subject: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"We’ve received your message – HOA\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n         HTML body: polite thank-you, copy of user message, SLA note.\\\\\\\\n   5.3 Configuration Set: contact-cs\\\\\\\\n       • Enables open/click tracking, metrics to CloudWatch, SNS on bounce/complaint.\\\\\\\\n   5.4 Suppression List Management\\\\\\\\n       • Automatically respect SES account suppression; events-fn records suppressed recipients.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n6. Bounce & Complaint Handling\\\\\\\\n   • SNS topic email-events-t subscribed by events-fn.\\\\\\\\n   • If complaint, mark sender email in DynamoDB (status=blocked) and future confirmation emails skipped.\\\\\\\\n   • Bounces update status; soft bounce retried via SES automatic handling.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n7. Delivery Tracking & Metrics\\\\\\\\n   • CloudWatch custom namespace HOA/ContactForm.\\\\\\\\n   • Metrics: Accepted, Sent, Delivered, Bounced, Complaints, ProcessingLatency(ms).\\\\\\\\n   • Dashboard created with graphs & alarms (BounceRate>5%, ComplaintRate>0.5%).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n8. Error Handling Strategy\\\\\\\\n   • Validation errors → 400 JSON {errorCode, message}.\\\\\\\\n   • Queue/Lambda errors auto-retried; after DLQ, CloudWatch alarm & SNS alert to devops@.\\\\\\\\n   • SES send failure captured, logged; message retained for manual inspection.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n9. Deployment & CI/CD Pointers (not in scope for immediate build)\\\\\\\\n   • Infrastructure as Code using AWS SAM or CDK (Typescript).\\\\\\\\n   • Stages: dev, prod; SES sandbox lifted for prod.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n10. Security Considerations Summary\\\\\\\\n   – IAM least privilege, Env vars encryption, WAF rules, CAPTCHA, rate limiting, input sanitation.\\\\\\\\n   – GDPR/CCPA: no sensitive personal data collected; 30-day retention of message data; opt-out supported via email.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n11. Future Enhancements (optional)\\\\\\\\n   • Auto-translate confirmation emails.\\\\\\\\n   • GraphQL API integration.\\\\\\\\n   • S3 archival of message content (encrypted).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeliverable complete.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:55:06.851Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{\"version\":\"claude-opus-4-20250514\"},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"generate","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}}},"displayName":"generate","startTime":1753149306908,"endTime":1753149411369.7417}